https://api.github.com/users/patwork/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patwork/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patwork/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patwork/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patwork/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patwork/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patwork/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patwork/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patwork/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2025-11-13T23:43:52Z","updated_at":"2025-11-13T23:43:52Z","body":"I'm sorry, I will clarify.\n\nMRP has two files containing an animated model of a character. One of these files is a source `Source\\Zorg.glb` file, which loads properly in dev2 and dev3.  However, the user created a second file as a scene (`Humanoids\\Zorg.tscn`), which contains a copy of all the data from the source `glb`, but was created using an old importer.\n\nThe difference is that dev3 needs to point the mesh specifically to Skeleton3D, which the importer does automatically. However, this does not happen when the mesh is hard-coded into the `tscn` file.\n\nIf `Zorg2.tscn` were an 'inherited scene' using data from `glb`, everything would work. In the worst-case scenario, it would require clicking 'reimport' on Zorg.glb.\n\nMy point is that, in my opinion it is not a \"**bug**\" and \"**regression**\", but \"**breaks compat**\" like explained in:\n\n> Animation: Remove default skeleton path in MeshInstance3D (GH-112267).\n> If relying on the default skeleton_path in some scenes, users should manually re-specify the parent node as the NodePath, or they can enable the animation/compatibility/default_parent_skeleton_in_mesh_instance_3d project setting to restore the pre-4.6 behavior.\n","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","pin":n